course: dictionary
lesson: 13289
title: Grammatically correct
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Grammar is the set of rules for how words fit together in a sentence. The word grammatically tells you that something is done in a way that follows those rules. «Sam's sentence is grammatically correct: "The cats are sleeping."» The verb «are» matches the plural «cats», so the rules are followed.
explain: The ending -ly turns grammar into an adverb, a word that tells you how something happens. Here it tells you how a sentence is right: it follows the rules. «The two sentences are grammatically different» means they differ in their grammar, such as their verb forms, not in their colour or length.
explain: A sentence can be grammatically correct and still make no sense. «Colourless green ideas sleep furiously» follows the rules of English, but ideas cannot sleep or be green. So check two things: is the sentence grammatically correct, and does it mean something?
fact: Grammatically means in a way that follows the rules of grammar.
fact: «Grammatically» comes from grammar, the rules for how words fit together.
fact: «Grammatically correct» means the verbs, agreement and punctuation are right.
fact: Teachers mark writing for grammar, so checking your sentences matters.
fact: A sentence can be grammatically correct but still not make sense, as in «Colourless green ideas sleep furiously».
exercise: Which sentence is grammatically correct? | «Yesterday we goes to the park.» | *«Yesterday we went to the park.» | «Yesterday park the we went to.»
exercise: «The two sentences are grammatically different.» What does this mean? | They have the same grammar, but one is longer | *They differ in their grammar, such as their verb forms | They are written in different colours
exercise: Which sentence is grammatically correct but has no sense? | «Cat the on sat mat the.» | *«Colourless green ideas sleep furiously.» | «The cat sat on the mat.»
test: «Mr Patel says the class must write grammatically correct sentences.» What does grammatically correct mean? | Written very quickly | *Following the rules of grammar | Written in neat handwriting
test: Which sentence is NOT grammatically correct? | *«The dogs barks loudly.» | «The dogs barked loudly.» | «The dogs ran home.»
exam: Maya says her sentence is grammatically correct but it makes no sense. Can both be true? | *Yes, a sentence can follow grammar rules and still not make sense | No, grammar and sense are always the same | No, only long sentences can be correct
